{"qid":"Q156199","names":["Electorate of Saxony"],"title":"Electorate of Saxony","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Electorate of Saxony was created in 1356 when the Golden Bull confirmed the Duke of Saxe-Wittenberg as one of the seven imperial electors; the electoral dignity passed to the Wettin dynasty's Ernestine line in 1423. Under Frederick the Wise (r. 1486-1525) the electorate sheltered Martin Luther after the Diet of Worms (1521), giving the Reformation crucial early protection. In 1547, following the Schmalkaldic War and the Capitulation of Wittenberg, Emperor Charles V transferred the electoral title and much of the territory from the Ernestine to the rival Albertine line under Maurice, so that thereafter the Electorate of Saxony was held by the Albertines. During the Thirty Years' War the Albertine elector John George I made peace with the Emperor at the Peace of Prague (1635), gaining Lusatia rather than losing land; the electorate endured until it was raised to the Kingdom of Saxony in December 1806 amid the dissolution of the Holy Roman Empire.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Boundaries of the electorate are well documented through imperial records, and the various territorial changes via partition agreements are traceable; the exact borders in some periods reflect complex feudal sub-enfeoffments that are not always mappable with precision.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Electorate of Saxony","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Rudolph II","nativeName":"Rudolf II","roman":"Rudolph II","start":1356,"end":1370,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null}